    "body": "Photo: © Roza | Dreamstime.com\nBy Keith Mwanalushi\n27 November 2026\nAlaska Air Group is doubling its dedicated freighter fleet as it looks to unlock cargo demand in Hawaii and build a more substantial international freight business.\nHowever, the challenge might be turning that additional aircraft capacity into sustainable revenue growth.\nThe airline plans to introduce four Boeing 737-800 converted freighters in the first half of next year, increasing its dedicated narrowbody fleet from five to nine aircraft.\nTwo are earmarked for Alaska Airlines and two for sister carrier Hawaiian Airlines, with the flexibility to allocate capacity across the network according to demand and operational needs.\nEach aircraft can carry approximately 50,000lb of cargo, giving the carriers greater scope to develop regional freight operations while connecting shipments with its expanding international network.\nFor Hawaii, the investment addresses what Alaska describes as an existing capacity constraint. Hawaiian Airlines has served the market for nearly 85 years, but its reliance on passenger aircraft has limited its ability to meet the full potential of the cargo market, according to Ian Morgan, VP cargo at Alaska Airlines.\n“By adding dedicated freighter service and leveraging Hawaiian’s brand, we can meet that demand while empowering a more interconnected, global network,” he told The Loadstar .\nThe new aircraft in Hawaii will support shipments between the islands and provide additional capacity for seafood, agricultural produce, and other locally manufactured goods.\nMr Morgan said the combined operations would help support local industries, particularly those requiring reliable, palletised transport, adding: “The freighter network will connect seamlessly with our widebody fleet,” he said.\nThe ability to move shipments under a single air waybill could give Hawaiian cargo customers greater access to international markets, although the commercial benefits will depend on the availability of onward capacity, competitive transit times, and the ability to attract sufficient freight volumes.\nThe opportunity is significant, given the geographical challenges of serving Hawaii’s dispersed island communities. Dedicated aircraft could provide greater consistency for larger shipments, while passenger aircraft continue to offer additional belly capacity.\nThe expansion also comes as Alaska Air Group seeks to make cargo a more significant contributor to its overall business. Its Alaska Accelerate strategy identifies cargo as an important source of revenue diversification. Alaska’s growth opportunities span cargo services in Alaska and Hawaii, the continental US, international operations, ACMI leasing, and other ancillary engagements, according to Mr Morgan.\nThe group has set a target of growing annual cargo revenue to $750m by 2030, having increased cargo revenue by approximately 60% since 2024, according to its September investor day presentation. Opportunities also include mail, international belly capacity, and broader network connectivity.\nBeyond its own network, the reference to ACMI leasing – providing aircraft, crews, maintenance, and insurance to third-party operators – could allow Alaska to generate additional returns from its freighter assets, although the airline has not detailed how much of its future fleet capacity might be allocated to such arrangements.\nMr Morgan said the objective was to reach new markets while maintaining the reliability of existing services.\n“Doubling our dedicated freighter capacity gives us more room to pursue opportunities in Alaska and Hawaiʻi, while giving us more flexibility to allocate aircraft where they are needed,” he added.\nThe challenge is ensuring the additional capacity attracts new cargo business rather than simply shifting existing volumes between more aircraft. Ultimately, Alaska’s success may rest on how effectively it connects regional markets to its expanding international network.",
